 ## Overview
-Mesh-adsorbed Gaussian (MaGS) is a hybrid scene representation that combines a triangular mesh with 3D Gaussians, where Gaussians are "adsorbed" to mesh facets rather than being rigidly anchored in free space [S1, unofficial]. This allows Gaussians to "roam" on the surface, increasing their degrees of freedom for movement and deformation [S1, unofficial]. It is designed to facilitate the reconstruction and simulation of dynamic 3D objects by providing a structured yet flexible primitive layout [S1, unofficial].
+Mesh-adsorbed Gaussian (MaGS) is a hybrid scene representation that combines a triangular mesh with 3D Gaussians, where Gaussians are "adsorbed" to mesh facets rather than being rigidly anchored in 3D space [S1, unofficial]. This allows Gaussians to "roam" on the surface, increasing their degrees of freedom for movement and deformation [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard 3D Gaussian Splatting by tying the spatial position of Gaussians to a parametric mesh surface defined by logical coordinates, enabling efficient dynamic reconstruction and simulation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
